  <data key="d0">Clock</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">In the context of Analog to Digital (A/D) conversion systems, the Clock serves as the fundamental timing signal source that regulates operational rates and sequencing. Often indicated by CLK and Q pins in circuit diagrams, this signal provides the necessary timing pulses to drive the overall conversion process. Its primary role is to control sampling intervals and the rate at which digital counters increment, ensuring that the timing of data acquisition aligns with the system’s requirements.

The Clock is typically integrated with control logic, which enables or disables the signal to manage the conversion cycle. When enabled, the Clock generates pulses that are directed to the counter circuits involved in the conversion. In a single-slope A/D converter, it specifically governs the increment rate of the counter. In a Dual-Slope A/D converter, the timing signal input drives the entire operation of the converter, including both ramp-up and ramp-down phases.

The termination of the clock signaling is an integral part of the conversion process, usually disabled by a comparator signal. This mechanism stops the timing process when the conversion is complete, allowing for the final counter reading to be captured. The counter reading is taken when the Clock is off, effectively freezing the count to represent the digitized value of the analog input. Thus, the Clock acts as the central timing reference, coordinating the interaction between the control logic, the comparator, and the counter to facilitate accurate analog-to-digital transformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00036-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00033-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00027-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00038-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00023-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00019-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00037-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00026-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790518759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Digital Output">
  <data key="d0">Digital Output</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The final digital result produced by the Counter in the Dual-Slope A/D Converter.&lt;SEP&gt;The discrete binary signal (generated by the counter) that represents the digital equivalent of the analog input.&lt;SEP&gt;The final digital representation of the analog input, comprising bits D0 through D3&lt;SEP&gt;The binary output (D0, D1, D2, D3, D4, D5, D6, D7) produced by the priority encoder representing the quantized analog input.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital result of the conversion, represented by bits D0 through D3, where D0 is the LSB and D3 is the MSB.</data>
  <data key="d3">chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00036-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00033-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00027-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00042-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00023-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
