</node>
<node id="Equal Impedance Condition">
  <data key="d0">Equal Impedance Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equal Impedance Condition is the general bridge equation derived from the balance condition, expressed as Z1*Z4 = Z2*Z3.</data>
  <data key="d3">chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00029-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790509464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Impedance">
  <data key="d0">Impedance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Impedance represents the complex opposition to current flow in the AC bridge circuit, denoted by Z values in the balance equations.&lt;SEP&gt;The total opposition to current in an alternating current circuit, consisting of both resistance and reactance.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrical properties in an ac bridge circuit that can be either pure resistance or complex impedance.&lt;SEP&gt;The opposition to the flow of alternating current in a circuit arm, expressed through complex mathematical formulas involving resistance and reactance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00029-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00035-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00027-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00036-chunk-000</data>
