  <data key="d0">Wheatstone Bridge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Wheatstone Bridge is a fundamental electrical circuit configuration primarily utilized for measuring an unknown electrical resistance value. Structured as a bridge circuit with two parallel resistance branches, each leg consists of two series-connected resistors. One of these legs incorporates the unknown component, conventionally denoted as R4, while the other legs contain known or adjustable resistive elements. The operational principle relies on achieving a specific balance state, which is attained by adjusting the known resistors until the current flowing through a central null detector, such as a galvanometer, reaches zero. This configuration effectively determines the unknown resistance when the ratio of resistances in one leg matches the ratio in the opposing leg.

Beyond its balanced operation, the Wheatstone Bridge functions in both balanced and unbalanced conditions. In an unbalanced state, a potential difference exists across the detector terminals, permitting current to flow through the connected galvanometer. This unbalance is critical for sensitivity analysis, allowing the circuit to quantify deviations from the equilibrium point. As a specific type of Direct Current Bridge, it is designed to handle DC circuits and is highly effective for detecting subtle changes in resistance.

The Wheatstone Bridge holds significant historical and theoretical importance in electrical engineering. It is recognized as the original electrical bridge circuit from which other specialized bridges, such as the Kelvin Bridge, are derived as modified versions to address specific measurement challenges, such as low resistance. Furthermore, it serves as the foundational method upon which all AC bridges are based, establishing the core principles of bridge-based electrical measurement that extend across alternating current applications.&lt;SEP&gt;An electrical circuit used to measure unknown resistances by balancing two legs of a bridge circuit, analyzed in the context of a slightly unbalanced configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00004-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00002-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00007-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00021-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00006-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00003-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00027-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00001-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00016-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790518907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
