<node id="Thevenin Equivalent Circuit">
  <data key="d0">Thevenin Equivalent Circuit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A circuit model used to simplify a complex linear circuit, characterized by a voltage source and a series resistance, calculated here as seen from the galvanometer's perspective.&lt;SEP&gt;A circuit concept used to simplify the electrical analysis of an Unbalanced Wheatstone Bridge.&lt;SEP&gt;The Thevenin Equivalent Circuit is a simplified representation of a linear electrical network consisting of a voltage source and a series resistor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00014-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00009-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00013-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790515120</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Galvanometer">
  <data key="d0">Galvanometer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Galvanometer is an electrical measuring instrument designed to detect and measure small electric currents. It is characterized by a moving pointer that deflects in response to current flow, allowing for the selection of standard movements such as a center-zero configuration. In the specific circuit contexts described, the Galvanometer often possesses an internal resistance of 125 Ohms and may be specified as a 200-microampere model, where the magnitude and direction of pointer deflection indicate changes in electrical resistance.

In the operation of a Wheatstone Bridge, the Galvanometer serves as a crucial null detector connected between the parallel branches of the bridge. Its primary function is to detect the balance condition; when the bridge is perfectly balanced and in a null state, the current through the instrument is zero. Conversely, in an unbalanced Wheatstone Bridge configuration, the device measures the current flowing through it during circuit analysis.

The theoretical determination of the current passing through the Galvanometer in such unbalanced scenarios is commonly performed using Thevenin's theorem. This analytical approach treats the Galvanometer as the load in the circuit, whose internal resistance is a defined parameter. By integrating these functions, the Galvanometer acts as a sensitive tool for both precise current measurement and the qualitative assessment of circuit balance in complex electrical networks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00014-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00002-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00018-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00008-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00005-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00009-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00007-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chap 3 DC&amp;AC BRIDGES::ch_00020-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790517522</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
