  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An analog-to-digital conversion method where the up/down counter counts up initially and counts down when the comparator detects the D/A output exceeds the input voltage, characterized by its tracking ability for time-varying inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00028-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790513151</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Digital-to-Analog Converter">
  <data key="d0">Digital-to-Analog Converter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A circuit that converts the digital value from the counter into a voltage, which is compared against the input voltage.&lt;SEP&gt;A Digital-to-Analog Converter (DAC) is a device that converts digital inputs into analog outputs, used in the context of measuring offset error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00028-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00008-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790516354</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tracking Converter">
  <data key="d0">Tracking Converter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A classification of analog-to-digital converters where the output counter tracks time-varying input voltages as they increase and decrease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chapter 5  ADC and DAC::ch_00028-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790513151</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
